## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to reason about lightweight on-device features under real-world constraints, including energy budgets, privacy regulations, and user consent requirements.

## Key Concepts
- Cost-benefit analysis under resource constraints
- On-device caching strategies and cache invalidation
- Energy-aware prefetching policies
- Privacy compliance and consent overhead
- Phased rollout strategies and success metrics

## Code Example

```javascript
const PREFETCH_CONFIG = {
  batteryThreshold: 0.25,
  network: 'wifi',
  cacheBudgetMB: 75,
  maxMemoryPercent: 15,
  retryIntervalMs: 30 * 60 * 1000
};

class AssetPrefetcher {
  constructor(config = PREFETCH_CONFIG) {
    this.config = config;
    this.cache = new Map();
  }

  async shouldPrefetch(deviceState) {
    const { batteryLevel, networkType, isCharging } = deviceState;
    return (
      batteryLevel > this.config.batteryThreshold &&
      (networkType === 'wifi' || isCharging)
    );
  }

  async prefetch(assets) {
    if (!(await this.shouldPrefetch(getDeviceState()))) return;
    for (const asset of assets) {
      const cached = await caches.match(asset.url);
      if (!cached) {
        const response = await fetch(asset.url);
        await caches.put(asset.url, response.clone());
      }
    }
  }

  async getAsset(url) {
    const cached = await caches.match(url);
    if (cached) return cached;
    return this.handleMiss(url);
  }

  async handleMiss(url) {
    return fetch(url).catch(() => getOfflineFallback(url));
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design and quantify edge ML systems under privacy and connectivity constraints, balancing energy, latency, and regulatory risk. It probes real-world trade-offs of federated learning with differential privacy in production environments.

## Key Concepts

- **Federated learning with local updates**: Devices train on local data and share model updates instead of raw data.
- **Secure aggregation and differential privacy**: Cryptographic protocols and noise injection protect individual contributions.
- **Energy and memory budgeting**: Quantifies the compute cost of on-device training.
- **Drift detection and validation**: Monitors model quality under privacy-preserving constraints.
- **Rollout strategy and offline fallback**: Ensures reliability when connectivity is intermittent.

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple local update sketch for FL
function localUpdate(weights, localData, learningRate) {
  const gradient = computeGradient(weights, localData);
  return weights.map(w => w - learningRate * gradient);
}

// Secure aggregation (conceptual)
async function aggregateUpdates(updates, privacyBudget) {
  const clipped = updates.map(u => clipGradient(u, MAX_NORM));
  const noised = addNoise(clipped, privacyBudget);
  return average(noised);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to scope CBAs for privacy-preserving on-device ML and quantify energy, storage, bandwidth, and compliance trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Federated learning
- Gradient compression
- Privacy/compliance overhead
- Cost modeling
- Rollout strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
function computeCBA(params){
  const {devices, energyPerDevice, uplink, cloud, retention} = params;
  const energy = devices * energyPerDevice;
  const total = energy + uplink + cloud + retention;
  const breakeven = total / Math.max(1, (params.revenuePerUser || 1));
  return {energy, total, breakeven};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
